oh it's not a costume i'm an elf well  technically i'm a human but i was raised  by elves your christmas season  probably started just like ours did  cozying up  [Applause]  and putting on our favorite christmas  classic elf  but we just learned something about this  holiday movie that totally blew our  minds  we always knew that elf was a special  kind of christmas movie  but we had no idea that almost all of it  was  real that's right one of the most  delightful classics of all time was made  like  almost no movies are and the behind the  scenes secrets will make your head spin  even more than 30 turns around a  revolving door  every day on set i thought you were way  too over the top  elf has cemented itself in our hearts as  one of the most beloved christmas movies  of all time  even though it's not even 20 years old  and while it might seem like this  perfectly heartwarming holiday hit was  destined to become a classic  it actually had some real trouble  getting off the ground and could have  been a very different movie if the  producers had gotten their way  for starters the studio originally  wanted a different saturday night live  comedian to star in the titular role  and that actor was none other than chris  farley  we'll be in touch the writer of the film  wasn't happy with that idea  so he actually turned them down and  waited over a year for another producer  to come on board  who had the absolutely perfect idea the  up-and-coming  saturday night live superstar will  ferrell trust me  they like you the sweater worked and  even though we know that farrell is the  perfect person to embody the childlike  wonder of buddy the elf  the rest of the world was not convinced  he had the chops to pull it off  fortunately the team were able to  convince them that he was the man for  the job  and his casting was official do it yeah  more hip  more hip god it might have helped that  they had piqued the interest of one of  the most successful directors in all of  hollywood  john favreau except wait a second  favreau only had one directing credit to  his name at the time  and the producers weren't sure about him  either it was only when they brought him  in to talk about the film that he was  able to prove that he knew  exactly how to bring elf to the big  screen in a way that lined up with  exactly the kind of movie that the  writer and producers wanted to make  so they had a director and a star it was  time to get elf  off the ground and it was john favreau's  direction that  really made elf the movie it is today  because without him  another director might have decided to  use a ton of digital effects  in order to pull off all of the north  pole scenes but when he first  entered the meeting he had three rules  that he wanted to follow  make elf look like a stop motion rank  and bass christmas special  make it a truly family christmas movie  and make it a timeless  classic in order for it to be timeless  they couldn't use any cgi  at all because favreau knew that in 10  years time the animation would look  outdated and old-fashioned  which is exactly why there's nothing  fake about elf  every single thing you see in the movie  is done for real and some is even more  real than we could have imagined  a lot of people down south don't believe  in santa claus what  because will ferrell and john favreau  had not yet become the massive  celebrities they are today  when it came to shooting all of the  scenes of buddy running around the  streets of manhattan for the first time  they could actually just go out onto the  street and run around  so the first thing we shot was the  lincoln tunnel  i was terrified for will nowadays will  ferrell  wouldn't have been able to go two feet  in an elf costume without getting mobbed  by  fans but back then they had a ton of  freedom  so they put him in costume grabbed a  cameraman and hit the bustling new york  city streets  which means that all of the people that  buddy interacts with  and all of the crowds who are just  trying to get where they're going  are actually real people and not paid  extras  those are real people those aren't  actors the most amazing of which is the  man in the red jogging outfit who just  happens to look  exactly like santa he wasn't placed into  that costume by the wardrobe people  the guy in the red jogging suit santa  sit  that guy was just there and luckily they  had one of the best improvisers in the  world playing buddy  who knew exactly how to jump into these  spontaneous situations and create those  whimsical and hilarious moments  but not all of the real aspects of  making elf were all fun  and games with nyc strangers in fact  once the time came to shoot all of the  scenes that take place in the north pole  things got a whole lot more complicated  see because they couldn't use the  computer graphics in order to shrink the  elves or make buddy look like a giant  they were presented with the unique  challenge of trying to figure out how to  do it all  on set and by set we do mean a giant  hockey arena in vancouver canada  and not the actual north pole you sit on  a throne of lies  they started by building sets that were  abominably tiny  so that the already tall will ferrell  would look larger than life when walking  around on them  it's much cheaper to shoot on stages in  canada and then they brought out a piece  of old hollywood movie magic  called forced perspective that no one  had used in decades  in scenes between buddy and the other  elves they would actually put farrell  a lot closer to the camera than the  other actors with sometimes up to 10  feet of distance between them  that way when you look at it through the  camera it appears as if buddy is  huge and the elves are tiny when really  they're just far away  it was like you were reinventing the  wheel things got even trickier when it  came to the part where buddy sits on  papa elf's lap  in order to make it look good on camera  they actually had feral  sit on a little stool that was placed  above a real-life child  who was lying down on a diving board the  actor who played papa elf  bob newhart was sitting on a chair 10  feet away in the background  now that is some serious movie magic  i think we ended up with 47 course  perspective setups which is  a tremendous amount the other very  important part of the world of elf  is definitely the influence of the  old-timey rank-and-bass stop-motion tv  episodes  especially the very famous rudolph the  red-nosed reindeer episode  i'll find homes for all those misfit  toys  john favreau wanted the north pole to  look exactly like that world  and he insisted that they include a  handful of real stop-motion sequences  which cost  a lot more and take up way more time  luckily they were able to get it all  done on time  and there is no doubt about it that the  stop-motion world of the north pole is  one of the most beloved parts of the  entire movie  why did you do that one about the elf  there are a few things about elf that we  aren't quite sure if are real or not  and the biggest one has got to be when  buddy pours maple syrup all over his  spaghetti while we may never know if  that  is actually will ferrell eating a pile  of tomato sauce with syrup on it  it is something we could see him doing  for real one thing we do know however  is that the gum that he pulls off of the  new york city fence and puts in his  mouth was  definitely placed there by the  production and not a feat of the bravest  and grossest  method acting that even jared leto might  cringe at the stunt where buddy tries to  put the star on top of the tree by  running full speed at it before  both he and the tree come crashing down  however that was all real  and done in one take they used something  called a texas switch  in which farrell walked just off camera  long enough for his stent devil to come  back  running in and take a flying leap at the  tree with total abandon  we love all of these behind the scenes  secrets from the making of this iconic  movie  it just makes watching it again all the  more amazing when you know  exactly how much hard work and ingenuity  went into the whole thing  and john favreau was right if they had  done what everyone in hollywood  does these days and just let cgi take  care of it  it wouldn't have retained all of its  christmas magic and would probably look  super outdated already thanks to the  amazing  team and favro's three rules for making  elf it can now stand the test of time  and continue to be one of the best  christmas movies ever  i wanted to make a movie that had the  potential to be a a classic that would  show every year  just when we thought we couldn't love  elf any more than we already do  learning all about just how much love  was put into the making of it  somehow makes the movie even more  heartwarming what about you  are you an elf fan or do you have  another all-time favorite holiday  classic  is there anything we missed about elf  that we just have to know about  let us know in the comments and don't  forget to subscribe to keep up with all  the latest videos from the things  you
 

